{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target's obstruction characterization is false: it asserts that in any minimal stable mixed configuration every vertex has exactly one opposite-colored neighbor, so disagreeing edges form a perfect matching. Stability only requires at most one disagreeing neighbor. A minimal stable mixed configuration can have a single disagreeing edge: take two copies of the 7-vertex graph with degree sequence (3,3,3,3,3,3,2), connect the two degree-2 vertices by an edge, and color the two copies different colors. The bridge is the only disagreeing edge; its endpoints have two same-colored neighbors, and all other vertices have three same-colored neighbors, so no vertex flips. This is a locally stable mixed configuration whose disagreeing edges are a matching of size 1, not a perfect matching, contradicting the claimed 'each vertex exactly one' and 'perfect matching of cross edges' structure.","counterexample":["Two copies of a 7-vertex graph with degrees (3,3,3,3,3,3,2), connected by an edge between the two degree-2 vertices; color one copy red and the other blue. The single bridge is the only disagreeing edge, and the configuration is stable mixed but not a perfect matching."],"premise":null,"premise_evidence":null,"successor_question":null}]}